/ref 1072 #uuid "65698589-f97d-56e6-8561-436b3f3f5011" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180183 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the fact that $4=\\frac{4}{1},$ we can write the expression as\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{1}+\\frac{4}{n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of $n$ by multiplying the top and bottom of the first fraction by $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{1}+\\frac{4}{n} & = \\\\ \\frac{n}{n}\\cdot \\frac{4}{1}+\\frac{4}{n} & = \\\\ \\frac{4n}{n}+\\frac{4}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4n}{n}+\\frac{4}{n} & = \\\\ \\frac{4n+4}{n} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(n+1)}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180350 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180350 #edb/ref 1061 18875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180350 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2e650778-2844-52ef-8b57-d9a72869e882" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180350 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180351 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180351 #edb/ref 1061 18876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180351 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "619ce5a9-f7df-5e65-b3c8-68102bc19437" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180351 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180352 #edb/ref 1061 18879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180352 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "833d312d-0148-58a3-bccf-4edf9a9c2020" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180352 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180669 #edb/ref 1064 "Remember that in order to add fractions with different denominators, like\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 4 + \\dfrac 1 8\n$$\n\nwe need to put the fractions over a **common denominator**, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{8} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{2}{8} + \\frac{1}{8} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt works the same in algebra! To add two fractions, we need to put each fraction over a common denominator. Then, we add the numerators." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180669 #edb/ref 1065 3377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180669 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f5b564ba-0934-5110-87ce-f04259821232" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180669 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324512 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{n+4}{4n}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324512 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324512 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "43113724-4737-55bd-be10-ea13f354c498" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324681 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{n+4}{n}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324681 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324681 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "557c2210-cde2-5a90-ab2d-278d391fa572" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325018 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{4(n+1)}{n}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325018 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325018 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7c80d65a-f4b4-5976-85e0-2d42b91e04f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325119 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2(n+1)}{n}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325119 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325119 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "86d696a3-51f6-5c74-b60a-ee777cf766f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325381 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{4n+1}{4n}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325381 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325381 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a5c3cf18-f2f8-5303-b271-47d390e13977" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326544 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186324512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326544 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186324681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326544 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186325018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326544 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186325119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175