}-\\frac{2y}{6y+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to put the fractions over a common denominator of $3x(6y+1),$ since there are no common factors in either denominator that we can exploit. \n\nTo do this, we multiply the top and bottom of the first fraction by $6y+1,$ and the top and bottom of the second fraction by $3x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & =\\frac{x+1}{3x}-\\frac{2y}{6y+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6y+1}{6y+1}\\cdot \\frac{x+1}{3x}-\\frac{3x}{3x}\\cdot \\frac{2y}{6y+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(6y+1)(x+1)}{3x(6y+1)}-\\frac{3x(2y)}{3x(6y+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(6y+1)(x+1)-3x(2y)}{3x(6y+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6yx+x+6y+1-6xy}{3x(6y+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+6y+1}{3x(6y+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516927 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "805d3373-2bfa-59ad-b9de-b827b3792b78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516927 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186338784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516937 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186516941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516937 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "96219574-7431-50c0-baf3-3503c3ab2b03" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516937 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516941 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186516927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516941 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9fadf4e2-4826-56c6-8d16-4e2b4f03bec4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516941 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186516785 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
